irwise differences' and 'prefers the lottery with the more favorable sum'. If the sum is directional, then only one direction makes sense. Given the ambiguity, the key is whether the individual considered the differences in outcomes and their sum. The reason requires evaluating the sum of outcome differences — which involves computing differences between each outcome in one lottery and each in the other. The relevant information is the outcome values and their differences. The decision reason can be applied because the outcome values and probabilities are provided, and the pairwise differences between outcomes can be computed.",
